{
  "dom_id": "step-q74152",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=5$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-x+30?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x=5$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-5)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $-1$ | $30$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $-5$ | $-30$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-5}={x}^{2}-x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-5)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-5)(x-3)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$",
